<p>The best neighborhoods in Houston depend on what you want most: walkability, historic character, big lots, new townhomes or a short commute. Inside the city, Montrose, the Heights and the Museum District lead for close-in living; River Oaks, Memorial and Bellaire are established, higher-end areas; and the East End and EaDo offer newer development near downtown.</p>
<p>This guide compares 13 inside-the-city neighborhoods: who each one suits, what housing you will find, the overall feel, and the flood questions to ask. Looking at Katy, Sugar Land or The Woodlands instead? See our guide to the <a href="/blog/best-houston-suburbs-for-families/">best Houston suburbs for families</a>.</p>

<h2>Best Neighborhoods in Houston at a Glance</h2>
<p>The descriptors below are general and qualitative. Prices, school assignments and flood risk vary block by block, so treat this as a starting point.</p>
